NNOOTTEESS
       The   data    transfers    performed    by    pprroocceessss__vvmm__rreeaaddvv()    and
       pprroocceessss__vvmm__wwrriitteevv() are not guaranteed to be atomic in any way.

       These  system calls were designed to permit fast message passing by al‐
       lowing messages to be exchanged with a single  copy  operation  (rather
       than  the  double  copy that would be required when using, for example,
       shared memory or pipes).

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       The following code sample demonstrates the use  of  pprroocceessss__vvmm__rreeaaddvv().
       It  reads  20 bytes at the address 0x10000 from the process with PID 10
       and writes the first 10 bytes into _b_u_f_1 and the second  10  bytes  into
       _b_u_f_2.

       #define _GNU_SOURCE
       #include <stdlib.h>
       #include <sys/types.h>
       #include <sys/uio.h>

       int
       main(void)
       {
           char          buf1[10];
           char          buf2[10];
           pid_t         pid = 10;    /* PID of remote process */
           ssize_t       nread;
           struct iovec  local[2];
           struct iovec  remote[1];

           local[0].iov_base = buf1;
           local[0].iov_len = 10;
           local[1].iov_base = buf2;
           local[1].iov_len = 10;
           remote[0].iov_base = (void *) 0x10000;
           remote[0].iov_len = 20;

           nread = process_vm_readv(pid, local, 2, remote, 1, 0);
           if (nread != 20)
               exit(EXIT_FAILURE);

           exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);
       }

